<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0042">Definition <span data-tag="0042">1.4.4.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. Suppose we are given objects $X,Y,Z \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and morphisms $f: X \rightarrow Y$, $g: Y \rightarrow Z$, and $h: X \rightarrow Z$. We will say that <em>$h$ is a composition of $f$ and $g$</em> if there exists a $2$-simplex $\sigma $ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $d^{2}_0(\sigma ) = g$, $d^{2}_1(\sigma ) = h$, and $d^{2}_2(\sigma ) = f$. In this case, we will also say that the $2$-simplex $\sigma $ <em>witnesses $h$ as a composition of $f$ and $g$</em>. </p>
</article>
